    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with **Christian Encarnacion-Strand** (66.53) and **Caleb Durbin** (72.14) serving as the focal points of the offensive attack. **Christian Encarnacion-Strand** benefits from a high-upside matchup against the southpaw Foster Griffin, with a massive +16% park factor and favorable winds providing a significant lift to his base projection. Meanwhile, **Caleb Durbin** remains a mandatory start; despite the defensive hurdle of an elite catcher, his matchup against Ryan Johnson (5.22 xERA) combined with favorable wind conditions creates an ideal environment for him to exploit the stolen base category, which remains our primary objective. **Trea Turner** (70.99) also projects as an elite play, as his position at the top of the lineup and a matchup against Hunter Brown capitalize on his high floor and elite sprint speed, pushing his efficiency score into the 70s.\n\nRegarding our roster construction, the most difficult decision involved **Josh Smith** (45.75), who narrowly missed the cut behind the more favorable platoon profiles of **Carson Benge** and **Christian Yelich**. While **Josh Smith** offers utility, the negative impact of his dynamic platoon split against Brady Basso ultimately rendered him a bench piece compared to **Kazuma Okamoto** (61.39), who received a substantial +10% park boost that elevated his value significantly. We continue to monitor the status of **Junior Caminero** (66.81), **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (60.95), and **Lars Nootbaar** (53.34), as all three carry \"Lineup Pending\" designations; while their projected scores are strong, their actual output relies heavily on confirming their placement in the heart or top of the order. \n\nWe must remain cautious with **Yordan Alvarez** (54.99), whose score is suppressed by a difficult matchup against Cristopher S\u00e1nchez. While his xwOBA remains elite, the combination of a high-skill opposing pitcher and a negative platoon split makes him a marginal start relative to his typical output. Similarly, **Gary Sanchez** (42.68) and **Dillon Dingler** (46.15) occupy the lower end of our starting rotation today; while they provide necessary positional coverage, the matchup against Kevin Gausman and Matthews respectively highlights the need for a volatile, high-ceiling performance to justify their inclusion over the depth provided by the bench, including **Jacob Gonzalez** and **Hector Rodriguez**.",
